WEBVTT
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:13.370
<v Different Voices>From PRI, Public Radio International. From PRI, Public Radio International. From PRI, Public Radio International. Public Radio. Public-- Radio International. Radio International. One more time.
<v Different Voices>来自PRI，国际公共广播电台。来自PRI，国际公共广播电台。来自PRI，国际公共广播电台。公共广播。公共——国际广播电台。国际广播电台。再来一次。

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:28.540
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:49.560
<v Ira Glass>A few years ago, I was putting together a story about a high school senior prom. And after the prom, I tagged along with two couples in a Ford Tempo, as they drove around, going to parties, talking, and joking, and singing along with the radio. We drove to pick up a friend of theirs at her house, but all the lights at her house were off, and nobody seemed at home.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，我正在制作一个关于高中毕业舞会的故事。舞会结束后，我跟着两对情侣坐进一辆福特Tempo汽车，他们开车四处转悠，去参加派对，聊天、开玩笑，跟着收音机唱歌。我们开车去她家接一个朋友，但她家所有的灯都关着，似乎没人在家。

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:55.000
<v Girl 1>Wait, this isn't her house. This isn't her house.
<v Girl 1>等等，这不是她家。这不是她家。

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.470
<v Girl 2>Yes, it is. Her car's right there.
<v Girl 2>没错，就是这儿。她的车就在那儿。

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:02.130
<v Girl 1>Really? I don't remember this being her house. Is it?
<v Girl 1>真的吗？我不记得这是她家。是吗？

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.510
<v Boy>You guys have been friends for such a long time, you don't even know where her house is, man.
<v Boy>伙计，你们都当了这么久的朋友了，竟然连她家在哪都不知道。

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.440
<v Girl 1>Hey, hey, this is her new house, all right?
<v Girl 1>嘿，嘿，这是她的新家，好吗？

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.620
<v Boy>All right?
<v Boy>好吧？

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:22.790
<v Ira Glass>A word now about doing documentary stories. When you're doing this kind of story, this is pretty much exactly the kind of moment that you dream of. Everybody's acting the way they act when there's no tape recorder present. It's intimate. It's alive.
<v Ira Glass>现在说点关于做纪录片报道的事。当你做这种报道时，这基本上就是你梦寐以求的时刻。每个人表现得就像没有录音机在场一样。它是亲密的。它是鲜活的。

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:50.320
<v Ira Glass>You feel like you're hearing these people as they really are even though there is a tape recorder and a boom microphone, a huge one, a foot and a half long, and a stranger. And to get to this point, you have to spend hours with people, get them used to you, get them to the point where they're actually bored with being recorded. And I tell you all this really only as a prelude to telling you this story.
<v Ira Glass>你会觉得你听到了这些人最真实的一面，尽管现场有录音机，还有一个吊杆麦克风——一个又大又长，有一英尺半长——还有一个陌生人。为了达到这种状态，你必须花几个小时和人们待在一起，让他们习惯你的存在，让他们达到对被录音感到厌烦的地步。我告诉你这一切，其实只是为了引出这个故事。

00:01:50.320 --> 00:02:20.730
<v Ira Glass>A while back, a guy named Joe Silovsky bought some old reel-to-reel tapes at a Salvation Army thrift store. And he took them home. And one of those tapes had exactly this quality tape on it, like in a really good documentary, that is, the people spoke the way that they really talk in real life. It was intimate. And it completely revealed what the people speaking were like.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，一个叫乔·西洛夫斯基的人在救世军旧货店买了一些旧的盘式录音带。他把它们带回了家。其中一盘录音带里恰好有这种高质量的内容，就像非常好的纪录片一样，也就是说，人们说的是他们在现实生活中真正的说话方式。它是私密的。而且它完全揭示了说话的人是什么样子的。

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:35.540
<v Ira Glass>And hearing that tape gave us the idea for today's program, that we're going to devote the whole show to three stories made out of found tape like this. Accidental Documentaries, we call them.
<v Ira Glass>听了那盘录音带给了我们今天节目的灵感，我们将用整期节目来讲述三个由这种现成的录音带制作的故事。我们称它们为“意外的纪录片”。

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:56.710
<v Ira Glass>Act One takes place 30 years ago in Michigan. Act Two happens 40 years ago in my hometown, Baltimore. And Act Three happens 25 years ago here in Chicago. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕发生在30年前的密歇根州。第二幕发生在40年前我的家乡巴尔的摩。第三幕发生在25年前的芝加哥。请继续收听。

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:19.310
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Berrien Springs, Michigan. The tape that Joe Silovsky found is from 1967. It came in one of those boxes. I don't know if you remember these boxes that reel-to-reel tape came in in the '60s, and '70s, and '80s. And it fits two hours of tape. It's not that big. It's nine-inch reel of tape, two hours of tape.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，密歇根州贝里安斯普林斯。乔·西洛夫斯基发现的录音带是1967年的。它是装在那种盒子里面的。我不知道你是否还记得60、70和80年代盘式录音带装的那种盒子。它能装两小时的录音带。没那么大。那是九英寸的录音带盘，两小时的录音。

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:48.000
<v Ira Glass>And the box had stamps all over it, and addresses written, and crossed out, and written. And what it was was a letter on tape between this family in Michigan, the Davis family, and their son, Arthur, Junior, who was in medical school in Loma Linda, California. And the tape is interesting because it documents pretty thoroughly life in this one American family.
<v Ira Glass>盒子上贴满了邮票，写着地址，有划掉的，也有重写的。那其实是一封录在磁带里的信，寄件人是密歇根州的戴维斯一家，收件人是他们正在加州洛马琳达医学院读书的儿子阿瑟二世。这盘录音带很有趣，因为它非常详尽地记录了一个美国家庭的生活。

00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:01.300
<v Mother>Hello, son. There isn't much news. Grace was over one day. And she was feeling so bad about her marital situation. So I gave her this reality therapy book.
<v Mother>你好，儿子。没什么新闻。格蕾丝有一天过来过。她对自己的婚姻状况感到非常难过。所以我给了她这本现实疗法书。

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:17.350
<v Mother>And by the way, I sent you one, son. You'll be getting it in the mail soon. I had it sent right direct to you. It isn't that I think you need it. It's just a new insight on mental health, mental illness, that I think is good.
<v Mother>顺便说一句，儿子，我也给你寄了一本。你很快就会在邮件里收到。并不是我觉得你需要它。这只是对心理健康、心理疾病的一种新见解，我觉得挺好的。

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:56.560
<v Mother>I probably told you about the chapter I read to Jimmy when we were riding to Mrs. Lester's voice lesson. And it was a chapter on nagging. Smart wives don't have to nag. And it was so interesting. Halfway through it, I realized that this was written for me because I do nag quite a bit. And here, it brought out that sometimes a person nags because they want to feel superior.
<v Mother>我可能跟你说过我读给吉米听的那一章，当时我们正要去莱斯特夫人的声乐课。那是关于唠叨的一章。聪明的妻子不需要唠叨。那真的很引人深思。读到一半时，我意识到这是写给我的，因为我确实唠叨得不少。书里指出，有时人唠叨是因为他们想感到优越。

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:31.790
<v Mother>Each time they-- well, here, I'll read it, son. "If you stop to think about it, you know perfectly well that this sort of nagging, that means nagging to get things done and things, isn't correcting the evil of what you complain. It's only making you feel important, virtuous, and superior. So for goodness sake, find some more constructive way of enhancing your feeling of virtue if it's so badly in need of being enhanced."
<v Mother>每次他们——好吧，在这里，我读给你听，儿子。“如果你停下来想一想，你心里很清楚，这种唠叨，也就是为了让事情做成而进行的唠叨，并不能纠正你所抱怨的事情的恶果。它只是让你感到自己很重要、很正直、很优越。所以，看在上帝的份上，如果你的优越感这么需要提升，去找点更具建设性的方式吧。”

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:54.770
<v Mother>Daddy has been very, very busy working in the basement. One of the machines is broken down. And true to form, it's taken him a long, long time to fix it. We're behind as usual on strips. Jack goofed, I guess, in his order of extrusions. And we are out of extrusions and won't be having them until Friday.
<v Mother>爸爸一直在地下室忙着工作。其中一台机器坏了。不出所料，他花了很长很长时间才修好。我们像往常一样在加工条材上落后了。杰克订购挤压件时搞砸了，我想。我们没有挤压件了，要到周五才能有。

00:05:54.770 --> 00:06:10.400
<v Mother>I made $50 in one week and a half. That's when I bought-- I bought your suit, and paid my tithe, and things like this. Well, son, I'll say goodbye. But thank you for your words of advice as far as my resting. And thank you for being concerned about my welfare.
<v Mother>我在一个半星期里赚了50美元。那时我买了——我买了你的西装，交了十一奉献，还有诸如此类的事情。好吧，儿子，我要说再见了。谢谢你关于我休息的建议。也谢谢你关心我的健康。

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:23.260
<v Mother>I really have gotten along quite well recently. I didn't feel too bad of depression last month. Well, here I go. We'll see you again. And I'll let someone else talk on the tape. Bye-bye.
<v Mother>我最近确实过得还不错。上个月我没觉得有多抑郁。好吧，我要挂了。我们会再见面的。我让别人在磁带里说几句。再见。

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:36.090
<v Father>Hello, Joe. How do you like this big tape anyhow? Isn't this pretty neat? I don't know if we ever get the thing filled up. And if we do get it filled up, I don't know whether you'll ever have the time to listen to it or not.
<v Father>你好，乔。你觉得这盘大磁带怎么样？这不是很整洁吗？我不知道我们能不能把它录满。即使录满了，我也不知道你是否有时间听它。

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:55.950
<v Father>Things are surely busy around here. They're really popping. I didn't get to bed 'til after 1 o'clock though. I was working downstairs, and I had work ready to take up to Jack's today, so we can get paid. We don't have any strips right now though. I guess I got a call for supper, so I'll come back later.
<v Father>这儿真是忙得不可开交。事情进展得非常快。不过我一直到1点多才睡觉。我在楼下工作，今天还要把成品带去杰克那里，这样我们就能拿到钱了。不过我们现在没有条材了。我想我听到叫我吃晚饭的声音了，我待会儿再回来。

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:39.460
<v Father>I heard a little story that I thought I would tell you, Joe. Mom reminded me at the supper table. I just got back from eating supper. There were a couple out. They were getting acquainted, shall we say, doing a little French kissing. And the fella said, "Oh, I think I got your gum." And she said, "No." She said, "I got a bad cold." So I'll leave you with that little thought for the time being.
<v Father>我听到一个小故事，我想讲给你听，乔。妈妈在晚饭桌上提醒了我。我刚吃完晚饭回来。有一对情侣出去了。我们可以说他们正在互相了解，做一点法式接吻。然后那个男的说：“噢，我想我吃到你的口香糖了。”她说：“不，我有重感冒。”所以，暂时就把这个小段子留给你吧。

00:07:39.460 --> 00:08:10.090
<v Father>Hi, Joe. I just got through talking to you on the phone. Little Kenny [? Jacques, ?] who I don't know if you remember him or not, but he's Quint [? Jacques's ?] oldest son, got married last March. And his girlfriend was here in school. And they were intending to keep it quiet until after school was out, but I guess he's pregnant now. So I suppose when you fool around, and do things like that, why, I suppose that's the price you're prepared to take. But I guess he's still in the Army.
<v Father>嗨，乔。我刚跟你通过电话。小肯尼——我不知道你还记不记得他，他是昆特的大儿子——去年三月结婚了。他女朋友当时在这儿上学。他们打算等毕业后再公开，但我猜她现在怀孕了。所以我猜当你胡闹，做那种事情时，你就要准备好承担代价。不过我猜他还在军队里。

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:34.310
<v Father>I saw him tonight in the drive-in. They look real happy together. She seems like a real nice little girl, almost too nice for him. He's a real nice fella, but he's quite mentally retarded, I think. At least, he may not be mentally retarded, but he has an awful time in school trying to figure out his ABC's. Anyway, that's the way it goes.
<v Father>我今晚在汽车影院看到他了。他们看起来很幸福。她看起来是个非常好的小姑娘，对他来说太好了。他是个很好的人，但我认为他有点智力迟钝。至少，他可能不是智力迟钝，但他学拼音字母时费了很大的劲。不管怎样，事情就是这样。

00:08:34.310 --> 00:09:03.130
<v Father>And she seems to be real happy with him. She went with Carl Hamel before. And she seems like a good Christian girl. She's got one hand that's deformed. I suppose that makes her feel like it'd be awfully for her to get anybody. So she probably thought she'd take whatever she could get.
<v Father>她看起来和他在一起真的很幸福。她以前和卡尔·哈梅尔在一起过。她看起来是个虔诚的好女孩。她有一只手是畸形的。我猜这让她觉得很难找到另一半。所以她可能想，能找个什么样的就找什么样的吧。

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:34.390
<v Mother>It was interesting as we visited Aunt Menta and Uncle Wendall's this weekend. We just had a real nice time. But I felt a little odd about going and suggested in a very small way that Art ought to call Menta. But sometimes he gets real negative about my suggestions. And I realized that it's because many times I perhaps force myself on him. So I didn't say anything until about 50 miles before we got there.
<v Mother>这周末去门塔阿姨和温德尔叔叔家做客挺有趣的。我们玩得很开心。但我对去那儿感到有点奇怪，并委婉地建议阿瑟应该给门塔打个电话。但他有时会对我的建议表现得非常消极。我意识到这是因为很多时候我可能强迫他接受我的想法。所以我一直到离那儿只有50英里时才开口。

00:09:34.390 --> 00:10:08.530
<v Mother>I really felt as though Menta should know we were coming. I should have written and told her. But I've left the communication with his family to Daddy. Well, he got a little bit vexed, and jumped out of the car, and went to the telephone, saying, "I hope you're satisfied now." But when he came back, Menta hadn't realized we were coming. And we were all grateful that we'd made the telephone call even though I didn't want to be right and say, "I told you so." Because it wouldn't have been nice to have walked in on them at a quarter to 10:00 at night.
<v Mother>我真的觉得门塔应该知道我们要去。我本该写信告诉她的。但我一直把和阿瑟家人的沟通交给爸爸。好吧，他有点恼火，跳下车去打电话，说：“希望你现在满意了。”但他回来时，门塔根本不知道我们要去。我们都很庆幸打了那个电话，尽管我并不想为了证明自己是对的而说“我早跟你说了”。因为如果在晚上9点45分不请自来，那太不礼貌了。

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:25.620
<v Mother>It was interesting how genuinely they accepted us. I don't know if I could be that gracious or not. Although they live such different lives. They had no plans for the weekend. We came. This was fine. And we ate what they had. And this was it.
<v Mother>有趣的是他们多么真诚地接纳了我们。我不知道自己是否能有那么大度。虽然他们的生活方式如此不同。他们周末没有计划。我们来了。这就很好。我们吃了他们家里有的东西。就是这样。

00:10:25.620 --> 00:11:13.150
<v Mother>But I think of if the situation were reversed and Ment and Wendall dropped in on us, how many times we have company over the weekend, we have to go to functions, and we have music lessons, and things like this, and our lives are so completely different. It's interesting that we just spent all day sitting without doing anything at all. And this is so unusual for us. And even though I don't suppose I could do it every week, it did seem good for one week, just to sit, and look at each other, and talk. Ment and Wendall are sweet people really. It's very difficult for me not to judge a person's Christian experience.
<v Mother>但我想，如果情况反过来，门塔和温德尔突然造访我们家呢？我们周末有多少次有客人，我们要参加活动，我们要上音乐课之类的，我们的生活完全不同。有趣的是，我们整天坐着什么也没做。这在我们看来太不寻常了。虽然我不认为我可以每周都这样做，但坐着，看着对方，聊天，这似乎对这一周很好。门塔和温德尔真是可爱的人。我很难不去评判一个人的基督徒经历。

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:42.370
<v Mother>Darlene was voicing her experience last night. Art was a little bit perturbed because her skirt was too short. And Darlene was telling me that, really, she, in a way, resented Daddy's religion because it seemed to be so void of actual feeling and love. And I felt rather bad this morning. I got up to have my worship. I neglected it over the weekend.
<v Mother>达琳昨晚正在表达她的心声。阿瑟有点不安，因为她的裙子太短了。达琳告诉我，在某种程度上，她真的很怨恨爸爸的宗教，因为它似乎太缺乏真实的情感和爱了。我今天早上感觉相当糟糕。我起床做礼拜。周末我忽略了它。

00:11:42.370 --> 00:12:15.490
<v Mother>And I got up to have my worship. And as I did, Daddy came in and sat down beside me. And I felt rather funny because I really feel that he does this as a duty more than anything else. And when I have my worship, I feel communication with God, and I enjoy what I'm reading. And when Daddy came in, there was just a-- I don't know. I suppose you'd call it an inner resentment.
<v Mother>我起床做礼拜。当我这样做的时候，爸爸走了进来，坐在我旁边。我感觉有点怪，因为我真的觉得他做这个更多是出于职责。而当我做礼拜时，我感到与上帝的交流，我很享受我读的内容。当爸爸进来时，只是有一种——我不知道。我想你会称之为内心的抵触。

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:53.480
<v Mother>I didn't know quite what to do. I was right in the middle of reading a chapter. So for the first time in my life, rather than to try to anticipate what he wanted and his needs, I just let him sit there. And I thought if he would say to me, "Could I have worship with you?" it would be fine. But he didn't. So I just continued reading and didn't say anything at all. He sat there for a little while, and then he left.
<v Mother>我不知道该怎么办。我正读到书的中间。所以这是我一生中第一次，与其试图去揣测他想要什么和他的需要，我只是让他坐在那里。我想如果他对我说：“我可以和你一起做礼拜吗？”那会很好。但他没有。所以我只是继续读，什么也没说。他坐了一会儿，然后就离开了。

00:12:53.480 --> 00:13:22.830
<v Father>Hi, Joe. That was sure nice to hear from you the other night. What I wanted to talk to you about tonight was I wanted to tell you about our power feed. This power feed that I made, we got that going now. So I got a hydraulic cylinder from Bimba, put that on there, and put some microswitches on there. And as the thing goes up, it pulls the chain around the sprocket.
<v Father>嗨，乔。前几天听到你的消息真是太好了。今晚我想跟你谈谈我们的动力进给装置。这个我做的动力进给装置，我们现在已经开始用了。我从Bimba买了一个液压缸，把它装上去，然后装了一些微动开关。当东西上升时，它会拉动链条绕过链轮。

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:35.290
<v Mother>I think that if Daddy feels a spiritual lack and feels a need, then he, himself, ought to be the aggressive one and fulfill this need in his own way.
<v Mother>我想，如果爸爸感到精神上的缺失和需要，那么他自己应该主动去以自己的方式满足这种需要。

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:49.280
<v Father>So put that sprocket on there. And as the cylinder goes in, it pulls the chain that goes around the sprocket, it pulls it up and pushes the drill down.
<v Father>所以把那个链轮装上去。当油缸缩回时，它会拉动绕在链轮上的链条，把它向上拉，把钻头向下压。

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:54.970
<v Mother>I would love to begin to have worship with Daddy, together, as husband and wife.
<v Mother>我多么希望能开始和爸爸一起做礼拜，作为夫妻一起。

00:13:54.970 --> 00:14:03.350
<v Father>And then when it gets to the other microswitch, why, it comes back. And as it comes back, we've got weights on the other side of the sprocket, pulling the chain in the other direction.
<v Father>然后当它到达另一个微动开关时，它就回来了。当它回来时，我们在链轮的另一边装了砝码，向相反方向拉动链条。

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:27.850
<v Mother>We certainly have begun to enjoy many relationships that are very, very fulfilling in our marriage. But as far as this worship is concerned, it seems as though I'm always the one that has to make the initiative, plunge and say, "Well, come, have worship with me," and select things that are inspiring. And I think this is wrong.
<v Mother>我们当然已经开始享受我们婚姻中许多非常有意义的关系了。但就这个礼拜而言，似乎总是要我主动，去推动，去说：“好吧，来，和我一起做礼拜吧，”并选择一些有启发性的内容。我觉得这是不对的。

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:38.430
<v Father>And this new compressor works like a dream. It really is doing a good job for us, which makes us very happy. We're real happy with the results we're getting so far.
<v Father>这个新压缩机效果好极了。它确实为我们做了很好的工作，这让我们非常高兴。我们对目前得到的结果非常满意。

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:59.660
<v Mother>I was reading an article last week, where we, so many times, try to fit people into our own pattern rather than to understand their background and appreciate their own pattern. And I think that he ought to fulfill his spiritual needs in his own way.
<v Mother>上周我读了一篇文章，说我们很多时候试图让人们适应我们自己的模式，而不是去理解他们的背景并欣赏他们自己的模式。我觉得他应该以他自己的方式来满足他的精神需求。

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:15.580
<v Father>Ken is going to come over. Ken Scholski. I don't know whether you know him or not. But he does real good at drilling the big holes. So he's going to come over and drill a couple hundred of the strips with the big holes for us. So we can at least get into production again by tomorrow night.
<v Father>肯要过来了。肯·斯科尔斯基。我不知道你认不认识他。但他钻大孔很在行。所以他要过来帮我们钻几百个带大孔的条材。这样我们至少到明天晚上就能重新开始生产了。

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:25.330
<v Mother>Because I don't really know the depths of his spirituality and his basic needs.
<v Mother>因为我真的不知道他精神世界的深度和他基本的需求。

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:30.120
<v Father>This one is practically foolproof, as long as we don't put too much air pressure on it and drive it too hard.
<v Father>这个简直是万无一失的，只要我们不加太大的气压，不让它运行得太猛。

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.570
<v Mother>So because I have no way of knowing, I'm going to stop worrying.
<v Mother>所以因为我无从得知，我决定不再担心了。

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:41.710
<v Father>We've been drilling some extrusions, 18-inch extrusions on the press with two holes at a time. It's been going pretty good.
<v Father>我们一直在压机上钻挤压件，18英寸的挤压件，一次钻两个孔。进展相当顺利。

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:53.160
<v Mother>Daddy's calling, so bye-bye, son. And I'll see you later. I just had to tell you, son. Daddy screamed and hollered. You may have heard him over the tape, "Honey!" And I was right in the middle of a sentence to you, so I didn't go.
<v Mother>爸爸在叫我了，所以再见，儿子。回头见。我不得不告诉你，儿子。爸爸尖叫大喊。你可能在磁带里听到了他喊，“亲爱的！”我当时正在跟你说话说到一半，所以我没过去。

00:15:53.160 --> 00:16:10.670
<v Mother>But when I ran down, here, he has this power feed on. You know Daddy. He is very, very slow at what he does. And everyone has about lost their temper at him because they've tried so hard to be patient. And day by day, they've hoped this thing gets fixed.
<v Mother>但我跑下去一看，他把这个动力进给装置装好了。你知道爸爸。他做事情非常非常慢。每个人都快被他磨光了耐心，因为他们已经尽力去宽容他了。他们日复一日地希望这东西能修好。

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:42.240
<v Mother>And so now I find this morning that he had fixed it. And this John that is working there was sitting there and working this just as easy as could be. And of course, everyone was really thrilled. And Daddy was so tickled. He was just as tickled as a little boy really.
<v Mother>所以今天早上我发现他修好了。那个在那里工作的约翰正坐在那儿，操作起来简直轻松极了。当然，每个人都非常兴奋。爸爸也很高兴。他真的像个小男孩一样高兴。

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:49.430
<v Father>So son, we love you. We'll see you. I want to save some room here for your lady love. Good night.
<v Father>所以儿子，我们爱你。再见。我想在这里给你的爱人留点位置。晚安。

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:52.930
<v Mother>Well, son. I don't know who's talked.
<v Mother>好吧，儿子。我不知道谁说了话。

00:16:52.930 --> 00:17:01.770
<v Father>So son, we love you. We'll see you. I want to save some room here for your lady love. Good night.
<v Father>所以儿子，我们爱你。再见。我想在这里给你的爱人留点位置。晚安。

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:16.220
<v Mother>If I had the money, I would have called you tonight. It's funny. When you have an emotional problem, you don't quite know where to turn. They can say, "Turn to the Lord." And I think this is true. And yet, we're so human. We want human help.
<v Mother>如果有钱，我今晚就会给你打电话。真滑稽。当你遇到情绪问题时，你不知道该向谁求助。他们会说，“转向主。”我想这是对的。然而，我们终究是人。我们想要人的帮助。

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:44.700
<v Mother>I just come back from taking a walk this evening, thinking, "If I could just talk with someone." And of course, you don't talk your problems out with too many people. And so I decided I'd turn the tape recorder on. And you could hear a negative [CLEARS HER THROAT]-- I'm trying to get a cold-- you could hear the negative side of the Davis household, which has been typical in the last few years.
<v Mother>我刚从今晚的散步中回来，心想，“如果我能和某人谈谈就好了。”当然，你不会和太多人谈论你的问题。所以我决定打开录音机。你可以听到戴维斯一家的消极面——（清嗓子）——我在感冒——你可以听到戴维斯一家的消极面，这在过去几年里是很典型的。

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:49.300
<v Mother>Here's Darlene. I could go on and on, and I know I must stop. Bye-bye, son.
<v Mother>达琳在这儿。我可以没完没了地说下去，但我知道我必须停下来。再见，儿子。

00:17:49.300 --> 00:18:18.710
<v Sister>Hi, Art. This week has really been a week, I tell you. I think it was Monday afternoon, I went into Mrs. Hare's office. And she told me that I would not be able to take my test because of my debit, which is $500-and-some. And that was very upsetting to me of course. I didn't realize I couldn't take my test for this.
<v Sister>嗨，阿瑟。我跟你说，这周真是糟糕透了。我想是周一下午，我去了海尔夫人的办公室。她告诉我，因为我的债务——500多美元——我不能参加考试。那当然让我非常难过。我没想到会因为这个不能考试。

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:45.550
<v Mother>There's no excuse for this really other than that I can see for the first time she has money of her own. And it's the little things that she's bought. It seems good to just have money and spend it. And at the first of the year, she really probably didn't work quite as much as she should've. Regardless of what's gone over the dam, she's left with a $500 bill now. And well, naturally, we brought the problem to Daddy.
<v Mother>这真的没什么借口，除了我第一次看到她有自己的钱了。那是她买的一些小东西。手里有钱花感觉真好。在年初，她可能确实没有像她应该做的那样工作。不管过去发生了什么，她现在剩下了一张500美元的账单。好吧，自然，我们把问题带给了爸爸。

00:18:45.550 --> 00:19:02.160
<v Sister>First, I talked with Dad about it. And he said he'd see what he could do. Well, he didn't do anything. There just seems to be no understanding at all there. But I guess that's to be expected, being what Daddy's like.
<v Sister>首先，我和爸爸谈了这件事。他说他会看看能做什么。好吧，他什么也没做。那里似乎根本没有任何理解。但我想这是意料之中的，毕竟爸爸就是那样的人。

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:24.200
<v Mother>When Darlene found this out from Mrs. Hare, she came home and cried an hour while I was teaching music lessons. And after the lessons, I went up and found out what the problem was. Naturally, she realizes her mistake. She's defeated. She doesn't know which way to turn. And I explained this to Art last night that she had realized that she made mistakes.
<v Mother>当达琳从海尔夫人那里知道这件事时，她回家哭了一个小时，当时我正在教音乐课。课后，我上去了解了情况。自然，她意识到了自己的错误。她被击败了。她不知道该往哪儿走。我昨晚向阿瑟解释说，她已经意识到她犯了错误。

00:19:24.200 --> 00:20:10.300
<v Mother>But goodness knows how many mistakes we've made in life. And as parents, if we could just help her pick up the pieces and somehow put them together again, so she wouldn't feel defeated, this would be so much more rewarding. But I also told him that I felt that we hadn't really helped you children too much with your schooling and that it seemed to me, and I'm sure you children felt the same way, that there were hundreds of dollars that goes into the basement. And I didn't really think it was right. Well, this he resented very much.
<v Mother>但上帝知道我们在生活中犯过多少错误。作为父母，如果我们能帮她收拾残局，设法把它们重新拼凑起来，这样她就不会感到挫败，那会更有意义。但我同时也告诉他，我觉得我们并没有真正帮你们这些孩子太多学校里的事，而且在我看来——我相信你们这些孩子也觉得一样——有几百美元投进了地下室。我觉得这真的不太对。好吧，这让他感到非常不满。

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:48.240
<v Father>Mom said she thought I ought to help her on this bill. But I don't know whether I'm wrong in this situation or not, but I feel in a way that she had this coming because she hasn't put in her full time. This last month she has. But up until that time, she's agreed for 12 hours a week, and it's been usually around-- oh, I imagine 5 to 6 hours is a lot closer to what's actually been accomplished. And then the money she does get, she's been spending on so many incidentals. And I kept talking and talking, but it didn't seem to do much good.
<v Father>妈妈说她认为我应该帮她付这笔账单。但我不知道在这种情况下我是否错了，但在某种程度上，我觉得这是她自找的，因为她没有全时工作。这个月她工作了。但在那之前，她同意每周工作12小时，通常只有——噢，我想5到6小时更接近实际完成的工作量。而且她挣到的钱，都花在了很多零碎开销上。我一直说个不停，但似乎没什么用。

00:20:48.240 --> 00:21:12.540
<v Father>But I feel it's better to provide a way for children to earn their education than it is to actually give them the money. And besides, we don't have the money. And if we weren't working down in the basement, why, there wouldn't be any money any more than there is right now. In fact, there wouldn't be as much. So I don't know.
<v Father>但我认为，为孩子们提供赚取教育费用的途径，比直接给他们钱要好。再说，我们也没有那笔钱。如果我们不在地下室工作，那么就不会再有钱了。事实上，钱会比现在少。所以我不知道。

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:41.890
<v Father>I guess I get mixed up in my philosophy and my thinking sometimes. And I know Darlene sometimes resents the fact that we don't give her more money. She sees all these other folks getting money, and she doesn't. I don't know exactly what to do about the situation. I don't know what's really right and what's wrong.
<v Father>我想我的哲学和思考有时会混在一起。我知道达琳有时会因为我们没给她更多的钱而感到不满。她看到其他人都拿到钱了，而她没有。我不知道该如何处理这种情况。我不知道什么是真正对的，什么是错的。

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:57.580
<v Father>But right now, I'm pretty strapped with all the taxes. I just had to pay $400 out at the first of the month. And now I come here again in the middle of the month and have to pay another $200.
<v Father>但现在，各种税费让我压力很大。月初我刚交了400美元。现在又是月中，我又得再交200美元。

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:24.090
<v Sister>Just a minute. Back again. Dad just came up to talk to me for a few minutes. I didn't know if I was going to work for him this summer or not after our little mix-up here. But I guess I'm going to work at the school and then in the afternoons for Dad because I have to pay off this debt just as soon as I can.
<v Sister>稍等一下。我回来了。爸爸刚上来跟我谈了几分钟。在发生这起小冲突后，我不知道这个夏天我是否还会为他工作。但我猜我会先在学校工作，下午再去帮爸爸，因为我必须尽快还清这笔债。

00:22:24.090 --> 00:23:00.550
<v Father>Well, anyway, be that as it may, Rich told a story he was told about a little mama mouse that lived on the-- oh, there goes-- the thing is running downstairs now. I can hear it clicking-- told about a little girl mouse that lived on one side of the corn field. And the little boy mouse lived on the other side of the field. And one day, the little girl mouse in the autumn made a trip across the field. And when she got to the little boy's house, she said, "Help, help, I've been reaped. I've been reaped." They were reaping the corn.
<v Father>好吧，不管怎样，里奇讲了一个故事，他说的是关于一只住在——噢，那儿——东西在楼下运行了。我可以听到它在点击——讲的是一只住在玉米田一边的老鼠妈妈。小老鼠住在田的另一边。有一天，小老鼠在秋天穿过田野。当她到达小老鼠家时，她说：“救命，救命，我被收割了。我被收割了。”他们当时在收割玉米。

00:23:00.550 --> 00:23:43.450
<v Father>And old Darlene just laughed and laughed. But she couldn't figure out what she was laughing at. The more she talked, the worse it got. She couldn't get the connection to the whole thing. See, she said, she thought it had something to do with emptying garbage or something. I don't remember just what her statement was. Anyway, it really made it quite funny. Her naiveness made it funnier than ever.
<v Father>老达琳听了笑得不行。但她想不通自己在笑什么。她说得越多，情况越糟。她没法联系到整件事。你看，她说，她以为这和倒垃圾之类的有关。我不记得她当时具体说了什么。不管怎样，这真的很搞笑。她的天真比以往任何时候都更有趣。

00:23:43.450 --> 00:24:03.180
<v Father>I'm going to quit for now. I've got some big holes to drill downstairs. And tonight's Fugitive night. I haven't watched Fugitive for so many months, I don't know whether he's still afoot or horseback. So well, anyway, so much for that. It's really been nice to talk to you.
<v Father>我先不说了。我得去楼下钻几个大孔了。今晚是《亡命天涯》之夜。我已经好几个月没看《亡命天涯》了，不知道他还在逃亡还是已经骑马了。所以，好吧，就说这么多。和你聊天真是太好了。

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:35.660
<v Father>And I want to leave you with one really profound statement that the more you drill on these castings, the lighter they get. I want you to be sure if you don't remember anything else that you remember that. So son, we love you. We'll see you.
<v Father>我想留给你一句非常深刻的话，你在这些铸件上钻得越多，它们就越轻。如果什么都不记得，我一定要你记得这一点。所以儿子，我们爱你。再见。

00:24:35.660 --> 00:26:39.880
<v Father>[MUSIC - "IT'S A MAN'S WORLD" BY JAMES BROWN]
<v Father>[音乐 - 詹姆斯·布朗的《It's a Man's World》]

00:26:39.880 --> 00:27:11.420
<v Ira Glass>Well, Alix Spiegel, on our radio staff, did a few days of detective work and tracked down the Davis family, who mailed this tape to their son in medical school in the late 1960s. And we reached the former medical student, Arthur Davis, Junior, in his home in Indio, California, not far from Palm Springs. He practices family medicine. He actually makes house calls, he said, and has six kids of his own. And he told us that these letters on tape went back and forth between him and his parents for a period of about four years when he was away in California in medical school.
<v Ira Glass>我们电台的工作人员艾丽克斯·斯皮格尔做了几天的调查工作，找到了戴维斯一家，他们在20世纪60年代末把这盘录音带寄给了在医学院读书的儿子。我们联系上了那位前医学生阿瑟·戴维斯二世，他在加州因迪奥的家中，离棕榈泉不远。他是一名家庭医生。他说他甚至会去出诊，而且有六个自己的孩子。他说，这些录音带信件往来于他和父母之间，持续了大约四年，当时他正在加州上医学院。

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:25.270
<v Ira Glass>He said, no, he hadn't heard one since the 1960s. He said, no, he had no idea how one could end up in a thrift store in Chicago. No, he said, his parents were not still alive. His mother died 12 years ago, and his father died 4 years ago.
<v Ira Glass>他说，不，他从60年代起就没听过一盘。他说，不，他根本不知道这东西怎么会流落到芝加哥的旧货店。他说，不，他的父母已经不在人世了。他的母亲12年前去世，父亲4年前去世。

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:58.990
<v Ira Glass>We sent him uncut tapes of everything that was on the tape. And he heard them. And he said that it was fine with him for us to play the tapes on the radio. He said that the tapes captured the dynamics of his family perfectly. The drama of a lot of American families is the emotional distance of the father, the father staying away from the family orbit, the father not being around, the father holding himself apart. And Arthur Davis, Junior says that his father was like a lot of American dads in that way and was, in fact, pretty removed.
<v Ira Glass>我们把录音带上所有内容的完整备份寄给了他。他听了。他说，他完全同意我们在广播里播放这些录音带。他说，录音带完美地捕捉了他家庭的动态。许多美国家庭的戏剧性在于父亲的情感疏离，父亲游离于家庭轨道之外，父亲不在身边，父亲保持距离。阿瑟·戴维斯二世说，他的父亲在这一点上和许多美国父亲一样，事实上，确实相当疏远。

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:29.030
<v Arthur Davis>He was reared in a divorced home. And there was a lot of bitterness. And so it was pretty tough for him to even consider getting married. And then when I was born, my mom said that he just broke into tears, thinking that he might have to deal with some of those issues as a parent. He never did really want to be a parent. And she really helped him through that a lot.
<v Arthur Davis>他是在单亲家庭长大的。有很多痛苦。所以即使考虑结婚对他来说也相当艰难。后来我出生时，我妈妈说他竟然流下了眼泪，想到他作为一个父亲可能要处理其中的一些问题。他从未真正想成为一名父亲。而她真的帮他克服了很多。

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:50.510
<v Arthur Davis>It was very fascinating, Ira. After my mom died, my dad changed tremendously. And he came to live with me, and spent quite a bit of time with my sister and me, and was very connected with us and our children. So that all changed after Mom died.
<v Arthur Davis>这非常迷人，艾拉。我母亲去世后，我父亲发生了巨大的变化。他来和我住在一起，花了很多时间和我和我姐姐在一起，与我们和我们的孩子建立了紧密的联系。所以这一切在母亲去世后都改变了。

00:28:50.510 --> 00:30:00.250
<v Ira Glass>Arthur Davis, Junior. Coming up, more old tapes, more about fathers in a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>阿瑟·戴维斯二世。接下来，会有更多的旧录音带，更多关于父亲的故事，稍后请继续收听来自国际公共广播电台的节目。

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:30.810
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of writers and performers to tackle that theme with radio monologues, original reporting, memoirs, short fiction, anything they can think of. Today's program, Accidental Documentaries. Stories made from old tapes that have been sitting in basements, and attics, and thrift stores for decades. We have arrived at Act Two of our program, Baltimore.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，邀请各种作家和表演者通过广播独白、原创报道、回忆录、短篇小说等任何他们能想到的方式来探讨这个主题。今天的节目是“意外的纪录片”。这些故事是由几十年来一直存放在地下室、阁楼和旧货店里的旧录音带制作而成的。我们现在进入了节目的第二幕，巴尔的摩。

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:59.820
<v Barry Glass>We'd like to ask you this question, my friend. Do personal problems and worries have you down? Are you disturbed by business problems, marriage problems, or emotional problems? See Mrs. K, reader and adviser. Mrs. K, formerly of Europe, gives you a reading and answers all your questions for just $1. And you'll feel much better. You'll have a much better picture of where you're headed after you've talk with Mrs. K. The telephone number is Saratoga 7-2753, but, of course, no appointment is necessary.
<v Barry Glass>朋友，我们要问你这个问题。你是否因个人的问题和烦恼而感到沮丧？你是否被工作问题、婚姻问题或情感问题所困扰？请找K夫人，她是一位读心者和顾问。曾旅居欧洲的K夫人只需1美元，就能为你解读并回答你所有的问题。你会感觉好多了。与K夫人交谈后，你对自己未来的人生方向会有更清晰的认识。电话号码是萨拉托加7-2753，当然，无需预约。

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:26.290
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to stop this tape right there. I have three things to say about this tape. Number one, formally of Europe? Number two, of course, no appointment is necessary. She already knows you're coming. And number three, this is my father in 1956, three years before I was born. He's 23 years old.
<v Ira Glass>我要在这里停一下这段录音。关于这段录音，我有三点要说。第一，曾旅居欧洲？第二，当然，无需预约。她早就知道你要来了。第三，这是我父亲在1956年录的，那时我还没出生，还有三年我才出生。他当时才23岁。

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:56.970
<v Barry Glass>And coming up right now, news about a wonderful appliance from Norman R. Mitchell, yes? For the homemakers, as we said before. We're talking about an electric dryer. And my friend, if the lady in your house has her heart set on an electric dryer, here's some big news for you from Norman R. Mitchell. Right now, they are featuring the new 1957 Frigidaire Filtra-matic electric dryer at an unbelievable low price.
<v Barry Glass>接下来，是来自诺曼·R·米切尔关于一款美妙家电的新闻，对吧？正如我们之前所说，这是为家庭主妇准备的。我们谈论的是一台电动干衣机。我的朋友，如果你家里的女主人一心想要一台电动干衣机，诺曼·R·米切尔这里有个好消息要告诉你。现在，他们正在以不可思议的低价推销新款1957年款富及第Filtra-matic电动干衣机。

00:31:56.970 --> 00:32:30.930
<v Ira Glass>My father started in radio when he was 19, the same age I was when I started. He began at the college station at the University of Maryland, and, after graduation, got a job spinning records at a commercial station in Baltimore. Then he was drafted. And at the time of this particular recording, my father was actually in the Army, stationed in Virginia. But he wanted a career in radio so badly that every Sunday morning, at the break of dawn, he would leave his wife and his five-month-old baby, my older sister, and drive up to Baltimore to do a four-hour program.
<v Ira Glass>我父亲19岁时就开始从事广播工作，那和我开始入行时一样的年纪。他最初是在马里兰大学的校园电台工作，毕业后，他在巴尔的摩的一家商业电台找到了一份打碟的工作。后来他应征入伍。在录制这段音频时，我父亲实际上正在弗吉尼亚州服兵役。但他太渴望广播事业了，以至于每个周日黎明时分，他都会离开妻子和当时五个月大的婴儿——也就是我姐姐，开车去巴尔的摩主持一个四小时的节目。

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:47.020
