<v Barry Glass>18年多来，成千上万像你们一样的当地人一直信赖诺曼·R·米切尔家电的真诚、服务和实实在在的优惠。这三点是当今成功企业不可或缺的要素。

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:56.240
<v Ira Glass>My dad's paycheck for this four-hour Sunday morning program was $5.88. The most he ever made at a radio job was $90 a week.
<v Ira Glass>我父亲主持这个周日早晨四小时节目的薪水是5.88美元。他在广播工作中赚到的最高薪水是每周90美元。

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:09.620
<v Barry Glass>My friend, how would you like to get 21 beautiful greeting cards absolutely free? Plus details on how easy it is to earn $35 to $50 a week selling Liberty Bell greeting cards?
<v Barry Glass>我的朋友，如果能免费得到21张精美的贺卡，你想要吗？此外，我们还会提供关于如何通过销售自由钟贺卡，轻松赚取每周35到50美元的详细信息。

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:10.140
<v Ira Glass>Hey Dad?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，爸爸？

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:11.160
<v Barry Glass>Yeah.
<v Barry Glass>嗯。

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:16.790
<v Ira Glass>When you were doing radio, what did you like about it? What was the appeal of it for you?
<v Ira Glass>当你做广播的时候，你喜欢它的哪一点？它对你有什么吸引力？

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:33.330
<v Barry Glass>It seemed easy to do. Certain amount of, I guess, notoriety. It's good for your ego. People know who you are. I was a big man on campus at the University of Maryland.
<v Barry Glass>这看起来很容易。我想，还有一定程度的知名度。这对你的虚荣心有好处。人们知道你是谁。我在马里兰大学时可是校园里的风云人物。

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:50.970
<v Ira Glass>You're a good announcer. I didn't really know that. I've been listening to the way that you do the announcements. You're relaxed, and yet, you punch the main points. But you sound completely at ease, and you're convincing. You're doing ads for the hokiest products in the world, and you sound completely like you believe it.
<v Ira Glass>你是个出色的播音员。我以前真不知道。我一直在听你做播报的方式。你很放松，但又能抓住重点。你的声音听起来非常自如，而且很有说服力。你当时在为世界上最土的产品做广告，但听起来你完全相信它们。

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:53.660
<v Barry Glass>Really? What are some of the products? I've forgot about them.
<v Barry Glass>真的吗？有哪些产品？我都忘了。

00:33:53.660 --> 00:34:00.330
<v Ira Glass>There's a lot of appliances. And then there's one reader and adviser named Mrs. K.
<v Ira Glass>有很多家电。还有一位叫K夫人的读心者和顾问。

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:02.410
<v Barry Glass>That's right. I'd forgotten about her.
<v Barry Glass>没错。我都快忘了她了。

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:07.540
<v Ira Glass>Formerly of Europe.
<v Ira Glass>曾旅居欧洲。

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:30.390
<v Barry Glass>Just show them to your relatives, friends, neighbors, and the folks you work with, and see how quickly they order. You're actually doing them a favor by saving them money on Christmas cards. And such fine quality cards you've never seen in your life as Liberty Bell.
<v Barry Glass>只需要把它们展示给你的亲戚、朋友、邻居和同事看，看看他们下单的速度有多快。通过帮他们节省购买圣诞贺卡的费用，你实际上是在帮他们的忙。你这辈子都没见过像自由钟这样高质量的贺卡。

00:34:30.390 --> 00:35:18.580
<v Ira Glass>A lot of what it's like to be on the radio is just trying to sound relaxed when you're not. You're doing a show. You're not just talking to people. And in some of these tapes, I can actually hear my father struggling to sound relaxed. And these recordings give me this picture of him that I have never had in my life really. He seems so young and innocent. A guy in his 20s, doing this thing that I know so intimately myself, just sitting in front of a microphone trying to sound at ease, trying to sound like this relaxed old pro, not a care in the world.
<v Ira Glass>做广播的很大一部分工作，其实就是当你并不放松时，尽量让自己听起来很放松。你在主持节目，而不仅仅是在和人交谈。在这些录音中，我甚至能听出父亲在努力让自己听起来很放松。这些录音让我看到了一个我从未见过的他。他看起来那么年轻，那么单纯。一个20多岁的小伙子，做着我本人也非常熟悉的事情——就坐在麦克风前，努力让自己听起来很自在，试图装作一个经验丰富的老手，仿佛世间无忧。

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:50.850
<v Barry Glass>Yes, now you can have color in that kitchen of yours and really make it a true showplace for your family and your friends. And make it a lot easier to work in too with color, huh? And you can have this color in your kitchen all at the price of white. And of course, Frigidaire features the new 1957 Frigidaire refrigerators, ranges, washers, and dryers in four decorator colors at absolutely no extra cost to you. You have your choice of pink, yellow, green, or charcoal.
<v Barry Glass>是的，现在你可以让你的厨房拥有色彩，真正成为你家人和朋友眼中的风景。而且，有了色彩，工作起来也会更轻松，对吧？你可以在厨房里拥有这种色彩，而且价格和白色的电器一样。当然，富及第推出了新款1957年富及第冰箱、炉灶、洗衣机和干衣机，有四种装饰色可供选择，且绝对无需额外费用。你可以选择粉色、黄色、绿色或炭灰色。

00:35:50.850 --> 00:36:02.480
<v Ira Glass>This is just how long ago this happened. They had not yet invented the idea of renaming the colors of appliances, things like avocado, lemon, eggshell.
<v Ira Glass>这就是事情过去多久了。他们当时还没有发明出为电器颜色重新命名的点子，比如鳄梨色、柠檬色、蛋壳色之类的。

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:36.910
<v Barry Glass>Well, let's get on to that featured top tune of tomorrow, a brand new release, one we think is going to be real big in the coming weeks and months all across the country and right here in Baltimore too. This week, it goes to The Crew Cuts. Listen to their rendition of "Love In A Home."
<v Barry Glass>好了，让我们进入明天的热门金曲推荐，这是一首全新的发布，我们认为它在未来几周和几个月内会火遍全国，当然也包括这里——巴尔的摩。本周，它属于“船员剪影”合唱团。请听他们演绎的《家中的爱》。

00:36:36.910 --> 00:37:11.690
<v Ira Glass>Not long after he got out of the Army, my dad decided that radio was no way to make a living. And after a series of jobs, he decided to become a Certified Public Accountant. He was a typical workaholic, suburban dad. Off to the office at 7:00 in the morning. Back at 6:30 for dinner. Exhausted after that, he would sit in his yellow recliner in front of the TV and fall asleep. He started his own business, struggled to establish it, worked lots of nights and weekends. During tax season, from January to April 15, we would barely see him.
<v Ira Glass>退伍后不久，我父亲就断定广播不是长久之计。在换了几份工作后，他决定成为一名注册会计师。他是一个典型的职场狂人、郊区父亲。早上7点去办公室。6点半回家吃晚饭。在那之后精疲力尽的他，会坐在电视前的黄色躺椅上睡着。他创办了自己的公司，努力打拼，牺牲了很多夜晚和周末。在每年一月到四月十五日的报税季，我们几乎见不到他。

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:12.700
<v Ira's Mother>What's this?
<v Ira's Mother>这是什么？

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:13.230
<v Ira's Sister>This.
<v Ira's Sister>这个。

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:15.420
<v Ira's Mother>Is that a radio?
<v Ira's Mother>这是一个收音机吗？

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:18.450
<v Ira's Sister>Radio.
<v Ira's Sister>收音机。

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:50.850
<v Ira Glass>In our house when I was growing up was an old reel-to-reel tape recorder, a little consumer unit, that my mother and my sisters and I would goof around on from time to time, record us singing or telling stories. And while my mom appears on these tapes a lot, my father does not appear on them once. He simply was not there to be recorded. The only time he's mentioned on all of these tapes is this. I'm going to play this moment to you. My mother is cooing to my older sister, Randi, who's probably-- I don't know-- a year old, a year and a half old.
<v Ira Glass>在我成长的过程中，我们家里有一台老式的卷盘式录音机，那是一台小型消费级设备。我和妈妈、姐姐们偶尔会用它玩耍，录下我们唱歌或讲故事的声音。虽然我妈妈在这些磁带里出现过很多次，但我父亲一次都没出现过。他根本没在那儿被录下来。在所有这些磁带中，唯一提到他的时刻就是这个。我把这个片段放给你听。我妈妈正在对大姐兰迪轻声细语，兰迪当时可能……我不知道……一岁半左右。

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:52.630
<v Ira's Mother>Where's Barry?
<v Ira's Mother>巴里在哪儿？

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:55.210
<v Ira Glass>Barry's my dad.
<v Ira Glass>巴里是我爸爸。

00:37:55.210 --> 00:38:05.250
<v Ira's Mother>Where's Barry, Randi? Daddy? Where's daddy? Did daddy go bye-bye?
<v Ira's Mother>巴里在哪儿，兰迪？爸爸呢？爸爸去哪儿了？爸爸是不是出门了？

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:07.100
<v Ira's Sister>Go bye-bye.
<v Ira's Sister>出门了。

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:08.850
<v Ira's Mother>Did he go outside?
<v Ira's Mother>他去外面了吗？

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:10.100
<v Ira's Sister>Go bye-bye.
<v Ira's Sister>出门了。

00:38:10.100 --> 00:38:11.380
<v Ira's Mother>In the car?
<v Ira's Mother>在车里吗？

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:12.360
<v Ira's Sister>Car. Bye-bye.
<v Ira's Sister>车里。拜拜。

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:19.330
<v Ira's Mother>Uh-huh.
<v Ira's Mother>嗯哼。

00:38:19.330 --> 00:38:24.710
<v Ira Glass>These days, my dad explains his decision to leave radio and become an accountant this way.
<v Ira Glass>如今，我父亲这样解释他当年离开广播界转行做会计的决定。

00:38:24.710 --> 00:38:49.350
<v Barry Glass>By that time, I had realized that radio was not for me. What happened would be a new program director would come in. And if you weren't the apple of that guy's eye, then you were out of a job, and you gotta go start looking for a job again. Even though that never happened to me, I could see it happening to other people. And I wanted to be in control of my own destiny, and I decided that it wasn't going to work out. Radio was not going to work out.
<v Barry Glass>到那时候，我已经意识到广播不适合我。通常发生的情况是，新来的节目总监上任。如果你不是那个人的心腹，那你就会丢掉工作，然后不得不重新开始找工作。尽管这种情况从未发生在我身上，但我能看到它发生在别人身上。我想要掌控自己的命运，我决定这条路行不通。广播事业是行不通的。

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:50.600
<v Ira Glass>And that was 1959?
<v Ira Glass>那是1959年吗？

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:51.200
<v Barry Glass>Yeah.
<v Barry Glass>对。

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:52.240
<v Ira Glass>The year I was born?
<v Ira Glass>我出生的那一年？

00:38:52.240 --> 00:38:52.930
<v Barry Glass>Right.
<v Barry Glass>没错。

00:38:52.930 --> 00:38:54.520
<v Ira Glass>Are those two things related?
<v Ira Glass>这两件事有关联吗？

00:38:54.520 --> 00:38:57.940
<v Barry Glass>Not at all.
<v Barry Glass>完全没有。

00:38:57.940 --> 00:38:58.950
<v Ira Glass>It sounds like they are.
<v Ira Glass>听起来好像有关联。

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:01.010
<v Barry Glass>No, they're not. No, they're not.
<v Barry Glass>不，没有。真的没有。

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:27.130
<v Barry Glass>Well, all the stars are on record, and all the records star on the Sunday Morning Carousel. Coming up for you right now, our featured top tune of the day. Number one in the record stores we visited this week in Baltimore. And judging by your cards and letters here at WIN, it ranks very high. The Prince of Wales himself, one Johnnie Ray, with "Just Walkin' in the Rain."
<v Barry Glass>好了，所有的明星都留下了录音，而所有的唱片都在“周日早晨旋转木马”节目中大放异彩。现在为你带来我们本周推荐的金曲。这是我们在巴尔的摩唱片店走访发现的第一名。从WIN电台收到的听众来信来看，它排名非常高。威尔士亲王本人，约翰尼·雷，带来《雨中漫步》。

00:39:27.130 --> 00:40:07.970
<v Ira Glass>Now the way a story like this usually ends is that I reveal that this is why I'm in radio, to make good on my father's legacy, to live the dream that he never got to live. But I have to say, the fact is, I never knew him as a radio deejay. I did not know that he had ever done radio the entire time I grew up. It was never discussed. The recordings of him doing this stuff were packed away in the basement. Nobody talked about it. I never thought of him as somebody who did anything but certified public accounting. If my mom ever mentioned this, I do not remember it.
<v Ira Glass>现在，这种故事通常的结局是，我会透露这就是我进入广播界的原因，为了实现父亲未竟的梦想，继承他的遗产。但我必须说，事实是，我从未见过他作为广播DJ的样子。在我成长的过程中，我根本不知道他曾做过广播。这件事从未被讨论过。他录制这些节目的磁带被打包放在地下室里。没人谈论它。我从没想过他除了做注册会计师以外还会做别的事。如果我妈提到过，我也记不得了。

00:40:07.970 --> 00:40:46.650
<v Ira Glass>And I certainly didn't grow up with any special feeling about radio. I could care less about radio. Like most Americans, my medium of choice was television. I got my first job with National Public Radio in Washington when I was 19 years old. And at that point, I simply wanted a job in the media. I was a freshman in college. If an ad agency had hired me that summer, right now, I would probably be doing Bud Light commercials. I can just see the whole thing laying out that way.
<v Ira Glass>而且我成长过程中肯定没有对广播有什么特别的情结。我一点也不在乎广播。像大多数美国人一样，我选择的媒介是电视。我在19岁那年获得了华盛顿国家公共广播电台的第一份工作。当时，我只是想在媒体界找份工作。那时我还是个大一新生。如果那年夏天一家广告代理公司雇了我，现在我可能正在拍百威淡啤的广告。我完全能想象出那会是怎样的一番景象。

00:40:46.650 --> 00:41:24.330
<v Ira Glass>And in 1978, when I started working at NPR, my parents absolutely did not want me to do it. It wasn't even a judgment call. They were completely against it for the next, I want to say, 10 years, maybe it was 15 years really. They simply saw it the way most parents would. They saw it as impractical. They worried that I would never make any decent money, never be able to support a family, essentially, the reasons that my father quit radio.
<v Ira Glass>而在1978年，当我开始在NPR工作时，我父母绝对不希望我这样做。这甚至不是评价的问题。在接下来的10年，或许是15年里，他们完全反对。他们就像大多数父母一样看待这份工作。他们觉得这不切实际。他们担心我永远赚不到像样的钱，永远无法养家糊口，这本质上就是我父亲放弃广播的原因。

00:41:24.330 --> 00:41:58.880
<v Ira Glass>When I first dug out these tapes of my father, about a year ago, I asked my dad if he ever wished he could still do radio. And he was completely unenthusiastic about the idea. In all my life, I had only seen his desire to be on the radio once. And this was actually a couple years ago. I was filling in as the guest host of Talk of the Nation, this daily call-in show NPR does out of Washington. I was doing that for half a year. And my father had never seen me host a radio show.
<v Ira Glass>大约一年前，当我第一次翻出我父亲的这些旧磁带时，我问他是否还想做广播。他对这个想法完全不感兴趣。在我的一生中，我只看到过他一次流露出想做广播的愿望。那其实是几年前的事。当时我正在担任NPR华盛顿节目《国家对话》的代班主持，我做了半年。而我父亲从未看过我主持广播节目。

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:43.050
<v Ira Glass>And he and my mom drove down to Washington from Baltimore to watch me do the show. And before the show, he asked me if he could read the news. And at first, I thought he was joking. And I don't remember exactly what I said, but I joked something back at him. And then later, as we got closer to going on the air, he asked again that he would like to read the newscast, the NPR newscast at the top of the hour, at the beginning of the show. And I realized he was serious. And I had to explain to him that I didn't have any authority over that, that the NPR newscasters, they wrote their own news, and they delivered it themselves.
<v Ira Glass>他和妈妈特地从巴尔的摩开车到华盛顿看我做节目。节目开始前，他问我他能不能播报新闻。起初，我以为他在开玩笑。我不记得我具体说了什么，我跟他开了个玩笑。后来，随着我们要上直播了，他又问了一次，说他想播报新闻，就是节目开始时在整点播报的NPR新闻。我意识到他是认真的。我不得不向他解释我没有那样的权限，因为NPR的新闻主播都是自己写稿、自己播报的。

00:42:43.050 --> 00:43:32.410
<v Ira Glass>Since then I've had him on our show a few times, on this show, including as an announcer, co-hosting a Father's Day show. He's coming around, I think. Lately, he's started saying that maybe I did not make such a mistake going into radio, that it seemed to be working out OK, which I'm glad for. I think one of the hardest things for any parent is to have one set of expectations for their children, and then be forced by their children to change them. That's really hard. I can't imagine what it would be like to see your kid doing something that you, yourself, decided wasn't worth doing.
<v Ira Glass>从那以后，我让他上过我们节目几次，包括让他当播音员，共同主持父亲节特辑。我想，他正在慢慢接受。最近，他开始说，也许我投身广播界并没有选错，事情似乎进行得很顺利，对此我感到很高兴。我认为对任何父母来说，最困难的事情之一，就是为孩子设定一套期望，然后被迫接受孩子改变这些期望。这真的很难。我无法想象，看到自己的孩子去做你本人认为不值得做的事情，会是什么样的感觉。

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:46.700
<v Barry Glass>And time for us to go out on a limb and predict big things for a new record. Our featured top tune of tomorrow. Here's a guy we feel pretty safe in predicting big things for. His name is Nat King Cole. Our featured top tune of tomorrow, "My Personal Possession."
<v Barry Glass>现在是时候让我们大胆预测一下一张新唱片了。明天的热门金曲推荐。这是一个我们很有把握预测会大火的人。他的名字叫纳特·金·科尔。我们明天推荐的这首歌是《我的个人财产》。

00:43:46.700 --> 00:44:47.490
<v Barry Glass>[MUSIC - "MY PERSONAL POSSESSION" BY NAT KING COLE]
<v Barry Glass>［音乐 - 纳特·金·科尔的《我的个人财产》］

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:57.890
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Chicago. Nora Moreno now joins us with some records, and tapes, and a story.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，芝加哥。诺拉·莫雷诺现在带来了唱片、录音带和一个故事。

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:22.810
<v Nora Moreno>This is an old 45. It's my father. He was a pioneer in Spanish-language broadcasting here in Chicago, a radio announcer in the 1950s and '60s. My dad's not playing the guitar or singing on this record. He's reciting poetry. This is what he sounded like when I was little.
<v Nora Moreno>这是一张旧的45转唱片。这是我父亲。他是芝加哥西班牙语广播界的先驱，也是上世纪五六十年代的广播播音员。我父亲在这张唱片里没有弹吉他或唱歌。他在朗诵诗歌。这就是我小时候他听起来的样子。

00:45:22.810 --> 00:45:25.450
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:27.770
<v Nora Moreno>"I knew love."
<v Nora Moreno>“我曾懂得爱。”

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:29.530
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:45:29.530 --> 00:45:32.500
<v Nora Moreno>"It was very beautiful."
<v Nora Moreno>“它曾是那么美丽。”

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:33.430
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:35.090
<v Nora Moreno>"But in me--"
<v Nora Moreno>“但在我心中——”

00:45:35.090 --> 00:45:36.660
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:40.680
<v Nora Moreno>"In me, it was fleeting and a traitor."
<v Nora Moreno>“在我心中，它转瞬即逝，像个叛徒。”

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:52.430
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:59.210
<v Nora Moreno>"It blemished what was glorious. But it was great because it was the first love."
<v Nora Moreno>“它玷污了那些辉煌。但它依然伟大，因为那是初恋。”

00:45:59.210 --> 00:46:20.820
<v Nora Moreno>It was hearing my dad recite poems like this that made my mother fall in love with him. It was a poem called "El Brindis del Bohemio" about Bohemians toasting in the New Year that did her in.
<v Nora Moreno>正是听到我父亲朗诵这样的诗句，让我母亲爱上了他。那首叫做《波西米亚人的祝酒辞》的诗，讲的是波西米亚人在新年聚会上举杯，正是这首诗打动了她。

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:26.200
<v Nora's Mother>"El Brindis del Bohemio." Beautiful.
<v Nora's Mother>“《波西米亚人的祝酒辞》。太美了。”

00:46:26.200 --> 00:46:27.940
<v Nora Moreno>Describe it. What does it say?
<v Nora Moreno>描述一下它。它说了什么？

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:40.790
<v Nora's Mother>Well, this poem talks about women, the mother, the mistress, the girlfriend.
<v Nora's Mother>嗯，这首诗谈论的是女性，母亲、情妇和女朋友。

00:46:40.790 --> 00:47:00.420
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:31.780
<v Nora's Mother>There was a soldier. There was a Bohemio. And there was another guy. And they were doing this toast. The best part was when this Bohemio-- the toast is for the mother, the one who carry him nine month, the one who cry with him and took care of him when he was a baby.
<v Nora's Mother>有一个士兵，有一个波西米亚人，还有另外一个人。他们正在举杯祝酒。最精彩的部分是当这个波西米亚人——这次祝酒是献给母亲的，那个怀胎九月生下他，那个为他哭泣，在他还是婴儿时照顾他的女人。

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:43.800
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:47:43.800 --> 00:48:00.410
<v Nora Moreno>I know what my mother was thinking when she heard this. Any man who can get so worked up about his mother had to be a special man. So she called the radio station. They talked on the telephone, went out, and, three months later, they were married.
<v Nora Moreno>我知道我母亲听到这些时在想什么。任何一个能为母亲如此动情的男人，一定是个特别的男人。于是她给电台打了电话。他们在电话里交谈，见了面，三个月后，他们结婚了。

00:48:00.410 --> 00:48:18.200
<v Nora Moreno>Now falling in love with the voice on the radio may sound a bit extreme, but consider this. My mother was 16, in Chicago for only a few months after having lived in Puerto Rico. It was her first time in the States. She barely spoke English and mostly stayed at home.
<v Nora Moreno>现在，爱上广播里的声音听起来可能有点极端，但考虑到这一点：我母亲当时才16岁，在波多黎各生活过之后，来到芝加哥才几个月。这是她第一次来美国。她几乎不会说英语，大部分时间都待在家里。

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:48.250
<v Nora's Mother>It was like being in a cage after living on an island with the sun shining and sleeping with the doors open and windows. And when I came here and went to that apartment, I feel like I was dead to life. I used to cry every night.
<v Nora's Mother>在岛上生活时阳光明媚，睡觉时门窗大开，到了这儿就像被关进笼子里一样。当我来到这儿并走进那个公寓时，我觉得自己就像行尸走肉。我几乎每晚都哭。

00:48:48.250 --> 00:49:03.440
<v Nora's Father>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Nora's Father>［说西班牙语］

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:31.560
<v Nora Moreno>I'm not even going to bother translating this one. Let's just say it's very romantic about the perfection of a woman's eyes. And this man, the one you're hearing, I never knew him. Sure, I heard stories about his radio shows, how he would produce talent shows, and have live performances, and bring in crowds from all over the city. People would come to be part of live radio. Women wanted to hear him and his poetry.
<v Nora Moreno>我甚至懒得翻译这一段。只能说，这关于女性双眸的完美，极其浪漫。而这个男人，你听到的这个人，我却从未真正了解过。当然，我听过关于他广播节目的故事，关于他如何制作选秀节目，如何举办现场演出，并从城市各地吸引人群。人们来到现场参与广播节目。女人们想听他的声音和他的诗歌。

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:58.060
<v Nora Moreno>But by the time I was old enough to hear him on the radio, he was reading the news and not poetry. And what I remember were the fights between them, my mother and father, about how he was never home, always late, too busy for us. His life on the radio was all consuming. The very thing that brought my parents together drove them apart. And I had fights with him too.
<v Nora Moreno>但等我长大到能听懂他广播的时候，他已经只在读新闻，而不读诗歌了。我记得的是父母之间的争吵，关于他为什么从不回家，总是迟到，为我们忙得不可开交。他的广播生涯消耗了一切。正是那个促成我父母结合的东西，最终拆散了他们。我和他之间也有过争吵。

00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:17.240
<v Nora Moreno>Once when I was 16, my dad came home after being away for a few days. And I asked him what he was working on that kept him away so much. It wasn't as if he was out of town. He just didn't come home. Well, he didn't appreciate it, got very indigent, and he beat me.
<v Nora Moreno>16岁那年，我父亲在外地待了几天回到家。我问他一直在忙什么，以至于要离家那么久。他并不是出差，只是不回家而已。他对此很不高兴，变得非常气愤，还打了我。

00:50:17.240 --> 00:50:41.540
<v Nora Moreno>Partly, he was defensive because he was seeing another woman. And partly, it was because of this culture clash. I was talking to him like an American girl, but he was born in Mexico. And in Spanish families, you never questioned your father. You never questioned your dad. In his mind, there was nothing more disrespectful than a child questioning the authority of her father.
<v Nora Moreno>一部分原因是他当时有了外遇，所以心虚；另一部分原因是文化冲突。我用美国女孩的方式跟他说话，但他是在墨西哥出生的。在西班牙裔家庭里，你永远不能质疑父亲，不能质疑爸爸。在他看来，没有什么比孩子质疑父亲的权威更不孝的了。

00:50:41.540 --> 00:51:25.010
<v Nora Moreno>When my mother found out about the other woman, she couldn't believe it and didn't. He would tell her in that same beautiful voice that he still loved her. He persuaded her that she was imagining things, convinced her that there was no one else and could never be anyone else but her, all in that voice, that romantic, seductive, tempting voice that she had heard on the radio. Finally, after another one of their many fights, after months of continuous abuse, my mother took a gun and shot my father in the mouth.
<v Nora Moreno>当母亲发现他有外遇时，她简直不敢相信，也不愿相信。他用那同样美妙的声音告诉她，他依然爱她。他用那声音说服了她，让她以为自己是在胡思乱想，让她确信除了她没有别人，也不可能有别人。那声音正是母亲曾在广播里听到的，那种浪漫、诱人、迷人的声音。最终，在他们无数次争吵中的又一次爆发后，在经历了数月持续不断的虐待后，我母亲拿起一把枪，朝我父亲的嘴里开了一枪。

00:51:25.010 --> 00:52:06.120
<v Nora Moreno>My dad survived the gunshot. And when the police came, he lied to them about what had happened, so they wouldn't take my mother to jail. He told them that he was teaching her to shoot when the gun went off. The cops didn't believe him, but he didn't want to press charges, and basically, there was nothing they could do. The strange thing is that, for a while, that whole experience brought them closer together. For a while.
<v Nora Moreno>我父亲在枪击中幸存了下来。警察赶到时，他谎称发生了意外，这样他们就不会把我母亲带走坐牢。他告诉警察，当时他正在教她射击，枪不小心走火了。警察并不相信他，但他不愿提出指控，基本上，他们也无能为力。奇怪的是，在那之后的一段时间里，这段经历反而让他们变得亲近了一些。只是在那一段时间里。

00:52:06.120 --> 00:52:31.180
<v Nora Moreno>A couple of years ago, I decided to meet my father in Mexico. We were going to celebrate Father's Day together. I don't really know why we picked Father's Day. We both understood that being a father wasn't something he did very well. And when I think about it now, I can't remember a time in my life when I recognized that day. The point is that he didn't show up.
<v Nora Moreno>几年前，我决定去墨西哥见我父亲。我们打算一起庆祝父亲节。我真的不知道为什么我们选了父亲节。我们俩都心知肚明，他并不是个称职的父亲。现在回想起来，我记不起人生中哪一次真正重视过那个日子。重点是，他那天没出现。

00:52:31.180 --> 00:53:23.170
<v Nora Moreno>It makes me sad because what I ended up doing was so much fun, and I knew he would have enjoyed himself too. I went to visit my uncle and cousins in Mexico City. We told stories, recited poetry, talked a lot about my dad. My cousin even brought out his guitar and tried to recite some of the poems my dad used to read. It was typical for my dad not to show up to things that included the family. Now that he's not really a part of it, it seems normal.
<v Nora Moreno>这让我很难过，因为我最终在那儿玩得很开心，我知道他其实也会喜欢的。我去墨西哥城探望了叔叔和堂兄妹们。我们讲故事，朗诵诗歌，谈论了很多关于我爸爸的事。我堂兄甚至拿出吉他，试着朗诵我爸爸过去常读的一些诗。对我爸爸来说，缺席涉及家人的聚会是很典型的行为。现在他已经不怎么参与家庭生活了，这反而显得很正常。

00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:39.690
<v Nora Moreno>My parents are divorced. My dad is in Texas, lonely and broke, dying of cancer. He's been out of radio for almost 20 years. When I talked to him on Monday and asked him about his poems, he couldn't remember the last time he had written one.
<v Nora Moreno>我父母离婚了。我爸爸在得克萨斯州，孤独而破产，正饱受癌症折磨。他已经离开广播界快20年了。周一我跟他通话问起他的诗时，他已经记不起上一次写诗是什么时候了。

00:53:39.690 --> 00:54:35.940
<v Nora Moreno>I have a picture of my dad on a stage with the backdrop of a Spanish dancer and a big band surrounding him. There's a woman sharing the stage with him. She is elegant and stunning. She has this long, flowing evening dress. This woman is not my mother, but she captures my father. He's bowing at the waist. She holds out a hand to him, and he clasps it with both of his and kisses it, gazing up into her eyes with complete romantic concentration. His attention is for her alone.
<v Nora Moreno>我有一张照片，照片上我爸爸站在舞台上，背景是跳西班牙舞的人和一支大型乐队。有一个女人和他同台。她优雅动人，穿着一件长长的飘逸晚礼服。那个女人不是我母亲，但她迷住了我父亲。他深深地鞠着躬。她向他伸出一只手，他用双手握住，亲吻着它，深情地凝望着她的眼睛，神情极其浪漫专注。他眼里只有她。

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:40.770
<v Ira Glass>Nora Moreno works in philanthropy at the Boeing Company in Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>诺拉·莫雷诺在芝加哥波音公司从事慈善工作。

00:54:40.770 --> 00:54:48.790
<v Ira Glass>Nora, to end our story about your dad and you, you asked us especially to get this one particular song called "El  Rey."
<v Ira Glass>诺拉，为了结束我们关于你父亲和你的故事，你特意要求我们选播这首名叫《国王》的歌。

00:54:48.790 --> 00:54:49.820
<v Nora Moreno>The king.
<v Nora Moreno>《国王》。

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:51.510
<v Ira Glass>Explain the significance of this song.
<v Ira Glass>解释一下这首歌的意义吧。

00:54:51.510 --> 00:55:02.170
<v Nora Moreno>Well, it's my dad's favorite song. And he sort of took it on as a theme for his life.
<v Nora Moreno>嗯，这是我爸爸最喜欢的歌。他把它当作了自己的人生主题曲。

00:55:02.170 --> 00:55:04.310
<v Ira Glass>Can you tell us why this would be his favorite song?
<v Ira Glass>能告诉我们为什么这是他最喜欢的歌吗？

00:55:04.310 --> 00:55:12.020
<v Nora Moreno>Well, it talks about a man who has no place. And regardless of where he finds himself, he continues to be the king.
<v Nora Moreno>嗯，它讲述了一个无处容身的男人的故事。无论他身处何方，他始终都是国王。

00:55:12.020 --> 00:55:15.610
<v Nora Moreno>[MUSIC - "EL REY"]
<v Nora Moreno>[音乐 - 《国王》]

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:23.970
<v Ira Glass>"I know very well that I am outside, but the day that I die, I know you will have to cry."
<v Ira Glass>“我很清楚自己是个局外人，但当我死去的那一天，我知道你们不得不为我哭泣。”

00:55:23.970 --> 00:55:25.670
<v Ira Glass>"Cry, cry, cry," they're singing.
<v Ira Glass>“哭吧，哭吧，哭吧”，他们在唱着。

00:55:25.670 --> 00:56:11.420
<v Nora Moreno>Yeah. "You will say that you never loved me, but you will be very sad. And you'll be that way forever. With money or without money, I always do what I want. And my word is the law. I have no throne, or queen, or anyone who understands me, but I continue to be the king."
<v Nora Moreno>是的。“你会说你从未爱过我，但你会非常难过。你会永远那样。无论有没有钱，我总是做我想做的事。我的话就是法则。我没有王位，没有王后，也没有人能理解我，但我始终是国王。”

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:13.270
<v Ira Glass>Have you actually heard him sing this song?
<v Ira Glass>你真的听他唱过这首歌吗？

00:56:13.270 --> 00:56:29.590
<v Nora Moreno>Oh, yes. He sang this at parties, family gatherings, in the morning. He sang this all the time.
<v Nora Moreno>哦，是的。他在派对上、家庭聚会上，甚至在早晨都唱。他一直都在唱。

00:56:29.590 --> 00:56:38.160
<v Ira Glass>What's so beautiful about it is the guy in the song is clearly-- he knows he's not really the king. He knows he's not--
<v Ira Glass>最美妙的地方在于，歌里的那个人显然——他知道自己并不是真正的国王。他知道他不是——

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:47.390
<v Nora Moreno>Well, actually, Ira, that's not really true. I think that what's truer is that he really thinks he's king.
<v Nora Moreno>嗯，其实，艾拉，那并不是真的。我认为更真实的情况是，他真的认为自己是国王。

00:56:47.390 --> 00:56:47.690
<v Ira Glass>No.
<v Ira Glass>不。

00:56:47.690 --> 00:56:54.680
<v Nora Moreno>Yes. It was a tribute to the way he lived his life. His way. You know, Frank Sinatra?
<v Nora Moreno>是的。这是对他一生处世之道的一种致敬。他行事的方式。你知道弗兰克·辛纳屈吗？

00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:58.100
<v Ira Glass>It's funny you say that. I was just about to say, this is the Spanish "My Way."
<v Ira Glass>你这么说真有趣。我正想说，这就是西班牙语版的《我行我素》。

00:56:58.100 --> 00:57:07.940
<v Nora Moreno>"El Rey."
<v Nora Moreno>《国王》。

00:57:07.940 --> 00:57:26.090
<v Ira Glass>Well, thank you Nora Moreno. Our program produced today by Alix Spiegel and myself with Nancy Updike, Peter Clowney, and Dolores Wilber. Today's show was first broadcast in 1996, one of the first episodes of our program. Contributing editors for this show, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, Paul Tough, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Music help from Mr. John Connors.
<v Ira Glass>好的，谢谢诺拉·莫雷诺。今天的节目由爱丽克斯·斯皮格尔和我本人制作，参与制作的还有南希·厄普代克、彼得·克劳尼和多洛雷斯·威尔伯。今天的节目首播于1996年，是我们节目的早期剧集之一。本节目的特约编辑是杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林、保罗·塔夫和顾问萨拉·沃威尔。音乐协助来自约翰·康纳斯先生。

00:57:26.090 --> 00:57:30.230
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:41.240
<v Ira Glass>Our website, www.thisamericanlife.org, where you can get our free weekly podcast or listen to old programs online. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站是 www.thisamericanlife.org，你可以在那里获取我们免费的每周播客或在线收听往期节目。《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:57:41.240 --> 00:58:05.840
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资金赞助鸣谢]

00:58:05.840 --> 00:58:14.220
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight for our program by Mr. Torey Malatia. I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ电台对我们节目的管理监制是托里·马拉蒂亚先生。我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周再见，带给你更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:58:14.220 --> 00:58:55.130
<v Father>Well, anyway, so much for that. It's really been nice to talk to you. And I want to leave you with one really profound statement that the more you drill in these castings, the lighter they get. I want you to be sure if you don't remember anything else that you remember that.
<v Father>好吧，无论如何，就到此为止吧。和你聊天真的很愉快。我想留给你一句非常深刻的话：你在这些铸件上钻孔钻得越多，它们就越轻。我希望如果你记不住别的事，一定要记住这一点。

00:58:55.130 --> 00:59:00.130
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>国际公共广播电台（PRI）。
